  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A warming trend will occur for the rest of this \nweek, and it should stay mainly dry until a cold front arrives \nthis weekend to bring the next widespread chance of rain.\n\nBy Wednesday, a broad upper level ridge will build eastward over\nthe Appalachian Mountains. This pattern should result in a\nwarming trend taking place for the remainder of this week.\nTemperatures will jump back into the 70s and 80s each afternoon\nfor Wednesday through Friday. Meanwhile, opportunities for\nrainfall will remain scant through this workweek with only a\ncouple frontal boundaries passing to the north. One of these\nboundaries should cross central Virginia on Wednesday afternoon\nthat may offer a chance of isolated showers and thunderstorms, \nbut the lingering dry air mass will likely keep the moisture\nalong and north of Route 460. South of Route 460, the ongoing \ndrought will combine with winds gusting up to 30 mph across \nportions of North Carolina and Virginia on Wednesday to increase\nthe risk of fire danger. The next notable chance of rain to \nprovide any relief from the ongoing drought may not come until \nthis weekend when a cold front approaches from the west. The \nmodels hint at more chances of rain during early next week.",
